1. **Explicit verification failures can still become PASS.** At [classify_rows.py:110](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/eval/kaggle/classify_rows.py:110), consistency checks only exclude patch-apply errors. On an otherwise complete row, `resolved=True`, `exit_code=0`, positive patch bytes, and `result_error="Required test node did not pass: t::x"` produces **`h=None, agent=PASS`**, including under `"Harness task failure"`. Every other recognized JUnit failure prefix reproduces this. Mark stock success accompanied by an explicit verification failure H-VERIFY; preserve stock `resolved` and legitimate `EXIT0_UNRESOLVED`.

2. **Malformed message fields bypass H or crash classification.** The truthiness check at [classify_rows.py:51](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/eval/kaggle/classify_rows.py:51) silently accepts `False`, `0`, `[]`, and `{}` in either message field. Otherwise valid rows reach `EXIT0_UNRESOLVED` without H. A recognized companion message also lets malformed evidence through under `"Harness task failure"`. Truthy non-string values instead raise `AttributeError`; the final reporting call can raise even after H was established ([classify_rows.py:132](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/eval/kaggle/classify_rows.py:132)). Validate both fields as string-or-None before inspecting or reporting them; malformed values should yield H-ROW.

3. **Malformed setup evidence reaches agent outcomes.** At [classify_rows.py:70](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/eval/kaggle/classify_rows.py:70), `setup_calls={}`, `setup_calls=""`, and `setup_calls=[{}]` all produce **no H, `EXIT0_UNRESOLVED`**. A `setup.py` entry with `rc=False` also passes because `False == 0`. Other malformed shapes raise exceptions. Validate the list, entry and nested subprocess shapes, and return-code types before classification. Preserve legitimate empty lists and documented not-run entries.

These are synthetic reproductions; they are not observed arm F failures.

